{"causes":["Born in Central Italy during a time of relative stability before the Roman Empire declined","Foligno was an important city in the Roman province of Umbria","The Christian church was gaining popularity and influence across the empire"],"effects":["He helped protect Foligno from invaders","The early Christian church spread rapidly throughout the empire as a result of his efforts","Felicianus' martyrdom contributed to the growth of Christianity during the 3rd century AD"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Christianity","sourceName":"Christianity","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Felicianus of Foligno","targetName":"Felicianus of Foligno","context":"The Christian church, under the leadership of bishops like Felicianus, played a significant role in spreading Christianity throughout the Roman Empire"},{"sourceSlug":"Foligno","sourceName":"Foligno","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Felicianus of Foligno","targetName":"Felicianus of Foligno","context":"The city of Foligno was protected by Felicianus, who helped defend it against invaders during his time as bishop"},{"sourceSlug":"Roman Empire","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"Felicianus of Foligno","targetName":"Felicianus of Foligno","context":"Felicianus' martyrdom took place during the reign of Emperor Decius, who was part of the broader Roman Empire"},{"sourceSlug":"Decius","sourceName":"Decius","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Felicianus of Foligno","targetName":"Felicianus of Foligno","context":"Emperor Decius was the ruler who oversaw Felicianus' martyrdom in 249 AD"},{"sourceSlug":"Central Italy","sourceName":"Central Italy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Felicianus of Foligno","targetName":"Felicianus of Foligno","context":"The region of Central Italy is where Felicianus was born, marking the beginning of his journey as a Christian leader"}],"places":[{"name":"Foligno","role":"patron saint city"},{"name":"Umbria","role":"Roman province"},{"name":"Central Italy","role":"region_of_birth"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Felician of Foligno","type":"Reference article","slug":"felician-of-foligno"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1343984","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felician_of_Foligno"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Saint Felician of Foligno (c. 160–249 CE) was according to tradition the first bishop of Foligno in Umbria (central Italy) and a Christian martyr of the mid-3rd century, whose legendary episcopate of over 56 years — from the reign of Marcus Aurelius to the persecution of the Emperor Decius — represents one of the longest claimed episcopal tenures in early Christian hagiographic tradition. If the legendary dates are accepted, Felician was ordained by Pope Victor I (c. 189–199) and served Foligno and surrounding Umbrian towns as bishop throughout the era of the Severan dynasty and the Crisis of the Third Century, dying as a martyr under the Decian persecution (249–251 CE) — the first empire-wide systematic persecution of Christianity.

According to the 'Acts of Saint Felician', he was born into a Roman Christian family, consecrated bishop by Pope Victor I, and spent his extraordinarily long episcopate evangelising the towns of Umbria (Foligno, Bevagna, Spello, Trevi, Montefalco). Arrested during the Decian persecution at approximately 94 years of age, he reportedly survived torture and was beheaded on 24 January 249 CE. His remains were venerated at Foligno, where he became the patron saint of the city. The Cathedral of Foligno is dedicated to him, and his feast day (24 January) was observed throughout medieval central Italy.

Felician is a representative figure of the 3rd-century Latin church of central Italy — the generation of bishops who built Christianity's institutional presence in smaller Italian cities before the Constantinian recognition of 313 CE. The historical verifiability of his specific dates is uncertain (the 56-year episcopate is almost certainly exaggerated in hagiographic tradition), but the social reality he represents — Christian communities establishing themselves across Umbrian towns in the 2nd–3rd centuries, building episcopal structures, and providing martyrs for the Decian persecution — is historically attested.","subjects":["Early Christianity","Church History","Martyrology","Classical Era","Italy","Hagiography","Roman Persecution","Medieval Era"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Felicianus of Foligno's legacy is one of remarkable resilience and devotion in the face of adversity, reflecting the growing influence of Christianity during the late Roman Empire.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
